<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { program }</p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a procedure or function appears in a class, it is
referred to as a method. A class can refer to any of the declarations in the surrounding
module. The methods of a class can refer to both the variables inside the class
as well as the variables in the module that surrounds it.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>As a module can, a class can use the <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>private</span></span> word-symbol to specify declarations
within the class that are private.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A class does not create any actual variables, procedures and
functions when it is defined. A class is a template for a type. To create an
“object” from a class, it must be instantiated, which means to give the class a
complete set of locations in memory. If that memory is static, the instance is
static. If the memory is allocated dynamically, the instance is dynamic.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442661"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.1<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Static
objects</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To create a static instance, the class is used to form a
type as follows:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span lang=FR
style='mso-ansi-language:FR'>type</span></b><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:
FR'> si = <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>instance</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>of</b> c;<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:FR'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Which can be used to form static instances of the class:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>var</b> z: si;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The class <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>c</b> is
not itself a type, nor can it be used as such. <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>si</b>, however, is a type. To use a class as a type, the compiler must
know if a static or dynamic instance is meant.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A static instance is static even if it is a local of a
procedure or function, or instantiated within another class. An instance does
not become dynamic unless it is allocated dynamically via <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>new</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442662"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.2<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Dynamic
objects</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To create a dynamic instance, the class is used to form a
type as follows:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>type</b> di = <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>reference</b> <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>to</b> c;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Which can be used to form a dynamic reference to objects of
the class:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>var</b> z: <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>reference</b> <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>to</b> c;</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Where <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>c</span></span> is a class. This
creates a special variable used to access the &quot;object&quot; that &quot;instantiates&quot;
the class. The actual creation of the object is done by the standard procedures
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>new</span></span> and <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>dispose</span></span>:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'>new(z); { create new object,
referred to by z }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code>dispose(z); { destroy existing object referred to by z }</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The definitions for the object referenced by z can be
accessed by the same notation as for a <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>joins</span></span>
module:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='margin-left:99.25pt;text-indent:-99.25pt;page-break-after:
avoid'>z.c := 1; { assign value to object variable }</p>

<p class=code style='margin-left:99.25pt;text-indent:-99.25pt'>z.x; { call
object procedure x }</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A reference variable can point to any object defined with a
compatible class. It can also point to no object at all:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code>z := nil;</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Just as a pointer variable can. Further, two references can
be compared, or as-signed, just as pointers can:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program p;<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class </b>c;<b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.<b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> z, q: <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>reference</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>to</b> c;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>z := <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>nil</b>; q := z;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>if</b> z = <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>nil</b> <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>then</b> { perform actions on nil }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>if</b> q = z <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>then</b> { perform actions on q = z }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>In fact, a reference behaves just as a pointer, but without
the need to specify a dereference (&quot;^&quot;). Anytime a qualifier
(&quot;.&quot;) follows a reference, it is understood that the object
referenced is being accessed.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Just as for pointers, a reference can appear in a <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>with</span></span> statement:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> c;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> z: <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>reference</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>to</b> c;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>with</b> z <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>do</b> { statements }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>This effectively opens up the scope of the object within the
code.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>References are treated as pointers with respect to monitors.
A reference cannot be exported by a monitor routine.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442663"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.3<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Class
parameters</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Classes accept parameters that are a subset of procedure and
function parameters. Only value and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>view</span></span>
parameters are accepted in a class parameter list.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> a(la: integer; b: char);</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> n(la): <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>array</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>of</b> integer;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>i:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    
</span>integer;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>procedure</b> addup;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> i: integer;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>a := 0;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>for</b> i := 1 <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>to</b> la <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>do</b> a := a+n[i]+ord(b)</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { constructor }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>for</b> i := 1 <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>to</b> la <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>do</b> n[i] := 0;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Class parameters are special in that they are specified when
the object formed from the class template is instantiated. The class parameters
are evaluated, and kept as long as the object exists. The constructor, the
destructor, and all of the methods of the class can access the class
parameters.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> a(la: integer; b: char);</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>.</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> n(10, ‘c’): <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>instance</b>
<b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>of</b> a;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>r: <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>reference</b>
<b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>to</b> a;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span></b>new(r, 20, ‘g’);</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Note that the actual parameter list for the class appears
where it is instantiated. For static instances, this is the variable
declaration for it. For dynamic instances, it is the <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>new</span></span> statement that creates the
object.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Typically, class parameters perform a “geometry” change
within the object. That is what makes a class parameter different from just
applying a method to it after its constructor executes. A method could not
specify the creation of the parameterized array <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>n</span></span>
above. However, class parameters have many uses, and be used to specify any
class configuration parameter.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442664"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.4<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Inheritance</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>class-declaration = 'class' identifier ' [
class-parameter-list ] ;' [ 'extends' class-name ';' ] block '.' .</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>expression = simple-expression [ relational-operator simple-expression
] .</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>relational-operator = '=' | '&lt;&gt; | '&lt;' | '&gt;' |
'&lt;=' | '&gt;=' | 'in' | 'is' .</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Classes can &quot;inherit&quot; the definitions contained in
other classes:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> x;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end.<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> y;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>extends</b> x;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { program main block }</p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>extends</span></span>
word-symbol is followed by the class that is inherited.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The meaning of this is that the new class receives all of
the definitions present in the inherited class, and can then add its own.
Objects have the special property that all references to a class are compatible
with any class that is built by inheriting definitions from its reference
class. Because class references can be freely assigned between compatible
classes, this means that references are effectively compatible with the classes
that it inherits, as well. This means that it is possible for some part of the
definitions for a referenced class to be non-existent. Pascaline can detect and
flag an error for an access to a missing definition in a referenced object
either at compile time or runtime.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a program needs to determine if the definitions it
needs to access in an object exist can use a special expression operator:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code>is-operator = expression 'is' class-name</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>is</b> operator is
true if the referenced object either is the indicated class, or is a class that
inherits from that class. In either case, it means that all of the definitions
from the class defined as the base of the reference exist.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442665"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.5<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Inherited
class parameters</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Derived classes can access all of the parameters that appear
in the base class. They can also add new parameters:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> x(i: integer);</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end.<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> y(b: char);</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>extends</b> x;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { program main block }</p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Here, class <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span>
has one parameter, <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>i</span></span>, and class <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>y</b> has two parameters, <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>i </span></span>and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>b</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When the class is instantiated, the parameter list given in
a parameterized variable or new construct must have all of the parameters given
for all the base classes of a derived class, from the ultimate base class to
the ultimate derived class, in left to right order:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> x(i: integer);</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>.<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> y(b: char);</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>extends</b> x;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'>.</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'>var cr: reference to y;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   
</span>ci(10, ‘c’): instance of y</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { program main block }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>new(cr, 20, ‘u’);</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442666"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.6<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Overrides
for objects</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Just as static modules can, classes can override inherited
procedures and functions.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> x;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>virtual</b> <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>procedure</b> y;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { constructor for x }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> z;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>extends</b> x;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>override</b> <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>procedure</b> y;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { constructor for z }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { main block for p }</p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The main difference for the use of overrides with objects is
that the instances of the original object keep their original procedures and functions,
and only derived classes have the procedure or function replaced. Static modules
have the original procedure or function replaced for all callers.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Just as for a module, the original definition of a procedure
or function can be accessed by using the <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>inherited</span></span>
word-symbol:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>inherited</b> y; { call
the original definition of member procedure y }</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Only one override can exist for the same method in a object.
The inherited version of a method can only be accessed within the object where
the override exists.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442667"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.7<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Self
referencing</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Within the code that makes up the object, its procedures,
functions and start and exit blocks, referring to definitions within the object
is done as for any module, dynamic or static. However, an object frequently
needs to access the reference that defines access to it, as well. This is most
common in the case where the object exists in a list of objects, and is done
via the word-symbol <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>self</span></span>:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program</b> p;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>class</b> list;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> next: <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>reference</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>to</b> list;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>procedure</b> next;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>if</b> <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>self</b> &lt;&gt; <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>nil</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>do</b></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>self</b>
:= next { next list item }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>;</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { constructor for list }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>begin</b> { main block }</p>

<p class=code><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>.</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>self</span></span> stands in
for the reference that the object being operated on is based from.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<h3><a name="_Toc251442668"><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.33.8<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Constructors
and destructors</a></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>If a constructor or constructor and destructor exists for a
class, they are executed when the object is created by <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>new</span></span>, or at the start of the
object’s definining block, and when the object is disposed of by <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>dispose</span></span>, or at the end of the
object’s defining block. The constructor is executed on a <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>new</b>, and the destructor is executed on &quot;dispose&quot;. Just as
for a static module, constructors give an opportunity to set up and tear down
an object.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When an object has inherited classes, it has a set of
constructors and destructors from all of the inherited classes. These constructors
are executed in the order of inheritance on <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>new</span></span>,
that is from base class to derived class. The destructors are executed in the
opposite order on <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>dispose</span></span>. This
gives each base class the ability to set itself up first, then when the
inherited class has its constructor executed, the inherited class is fully
initialized.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When an object is torn down, the derived class has the
ability to deactivate itself before the class it is based on is deactivated.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

</div>

<span style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:"Times New Roman","serif";mso-fareast-font-family:
Calibri;mso-fareast-theme-font:minor-latin;mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi;
mso-ansi-language:EN-US;mso-fareast-language:EN-US;mso-bidi-language:AR-SA'><br
clear=all style='page-break-before:right;mso-break-type:section-break'>
</span>

