style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>etfun:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    
</span>(fkey:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>                </span>funky);<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>{ function key }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>etmoumovg: (mmoung:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>              </span>mouhan;<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>{ mouse number }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>                  </span>moupxg, moupyg:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>integer); { mouse movement }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>etup, etdown, etleft, etright, etleftw,
etrightw, ethome,</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span><span
lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:FR'>ethomes, ethomel, etend, etends, etendl,
etscrl, etscrr,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:
FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>etscru, etscrd, etpagd, etpagu, ettab,
etenter, etinsert,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:
FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>etinsertl, etinsertt, etdel,
etdell, etdelcf, etdelcb, etcopy,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:
FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>etcopyl, etcan, etstop, etcont, etprint,
etprintb, etprints,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:
FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span></span>etmenu, etterm, etframe: (); { normal
events }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>{ end }</p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=code style='page-break-after:avoid'>end;</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>F.18<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Graphical Terminal Objects</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>All of the procedures, functions and other declarations in <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span> are also available in a graphical
terminal object. of the form:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>Class Graphicalterminal;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>var input, output: text; { input
and output files for this screen }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>er: evtrec;<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span>{ event record }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>{ procedures and functions from
gralib }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The complete catalog of procedures and functions from gralib
are available as methods in the graphicalterminal class. There are minor
differences, which are detailed with the procedure and function descriptions in
<!--[if supportFields]><span style='mso-element:field-begin'></span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>REF _Ref251353925 \r \h <span
style='mso-element:field-separator'></span><![endif]-->E.16<!--[if gte mso 9]><xml>
 <w:data>08D0C9EA79F9BACE118C8200AA004BA90B02000000080000000E0000005F005200650066003200350031003300350033003900320035000000</w:data>
</xml><![endif]--><!--[if supportFields]><span style='mso-element:field-end'></span><![endif]-->.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>graphicalterminal</span></span>
object is instantiated, it’s input and output files are automatically linked to
the standard <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>input</span></span> and <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>output</span></span> files of the program.
Thus, where specifying the input and output files in <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span> procedures and functions
is optional, in methods of the terminal object these files <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><i style='mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>must not</i></b> be specified.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The input and output files used by the terminal object are
made available as class members input and output. This allows them to be used
in non-method procedures (such as read and write).</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A terminal object also contains an event record, so it is
not necessary to specify an external event record in the <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>event</span></span> procedure.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>graphicalterminal</span></span>
object can be created as follows:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program p;<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>joins gralib;<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> ti: instance of gralib.graphicalterminal;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>begin</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>ti.home; { send cursor to home position }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>writeln(ti.output, ‘hello, terminal world’);</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>repeat { event loop }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>ti.event; { get next event }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>{ process events }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>until ti.event = etterm { loop until program cancelled }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>end.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Where <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>terminal</span></span> is the
terminal object. Terminal objects can be instantiated statically or
dynamically.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>Terminal</span></span>
objects contain their own state for the following:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst style='text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l2 level1 lfo24'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>6.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Location of cursor.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l2 level1 lfo24'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>7.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Current attributes and colors.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l2 level1 lfo24'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>8.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Current font and size.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l2 level1 lfo24'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>9.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Buffer working/display status.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l2 level1 lfo24'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>10.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Tab
stops.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l2 level1 lfo24'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>11.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Timer
numbers.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpLast style='text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l2 level1 lfo24'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>12.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Cached
pictures.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>However, each terminal object gets the complete state of the
module <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span> when it is created.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Having each terminal object possess its own state means that
each object can be writing to its own section of the screen in its own<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>mode. Each object can even have it’s own
private screen buffer, but this must be specifically selected using the <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>select</span></span> procedure.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When multiple terminal objects exist, and all have the
cursor visibility on, the cursor will remain at the last place it was
specifically directed to. This can mean that the cursor will rapidly appear to
swap back and forth between the active drawing terminal objects. This can be
improved by only selecting one of the terminal objects as having a visible
cursor. Typically, a the cursor should be placed to attract user attention to
where text is being entered, or if none is being entered, it should be turned
off.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Note that <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span> also
exposes a terminal object that is identical to the <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span>
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>terminal</span></span> object. It features the
same calls as <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>GraphicalTerminal</span></span>,
but does not allow access to the advanced features in GraphicalTerminal.</p>

<p class=code><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442735"><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:
none;text-underline:none'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>F.19<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Procedures
and functions in gralib</a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>See <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span> for the
basic text functions. These are all implemented in <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>For all of the following calls, If the screen file <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span> is not present, the default is
the standard output file. If the procedure or function is a method, neither the
input nor output screen file should be specified, since it is inherent in the
object.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function maxxg[(var f: text)]:
integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Returns the maximum pixel index x
in output surface file <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function maxyg[(var f: text)]:
integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Returns the maximum pixel index y
in output surface file <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function curxg[(var f: text)]:
integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Returns the current location of
the cursor, in pixel units, for x in output surface file <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function curyg[(var f: text)]:
integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Returns the current location of
the cursor, in pixel units, for y in output surface file <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure line([var f: text;]
x1, y1, x2, y2: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw line. Draws a line between
the point <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span>
to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>,
using the current line width, color and mode in output surface file <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure linewidth([var f:
text;] w: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set line width. Sets the line
drawing width at <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>w</span></span> pixels wide in
output surface file <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>. Use of an
odd number of pixels is recommended.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure rect([var f: text;]
x1, y1, x2, y2: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw rectangle. Draws a rectangle
whose opposite corners are <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span> and <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>.
Uses the current line width, color and mode in output surface file <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure frect([var f: text;]
x1, y1, x2, y2: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw filled rectangle. Draws a
solid rectangle, whose opposite corners are <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span> and <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>.
Uses the current color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure rrect([var f: text;]
x1, y1, x2, y2, xs, ys: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw rounded rectangle. Draws a
rectangle with rounded corners. The opposite corners of the bounding box are
specified as <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span>
to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>.
The ellipses that specify the rounded corners are xs and ys, which specify the
width and height of the ellipse. Uses the current line width, color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure frrect([var f:
text;] x1, y1, x2, y2, xs, ys: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw filled rounded rectangle.
Draws a rectangle with rounded corners. The opposite corners of the bounding
box are specified as <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span> to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>. The ellipses that specify the
rounded corners are xs and ys, which specify the width and height of the
ellipse. Uses the current color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure ellipse([var f:
text;] x1, y1, x2, y2: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw an ellipse. Draws an ellipse
bonded by a box whose opposite corners are <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span> to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>. Uses the current line width,
color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure fellipse([var f:
text;] x1, y1, x2, y2: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw a filled ellipse. Draws a
solid ellipse bonded by a box whose opposite corners are <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span>
to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>.
Uses the current color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure arc([var f: text;]
x1, y1, x2, y2, sa, ea: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw an arc. Draws an arc on an
ellipse, whose bounding box is <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span> to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>. The arc starts on the ellipse
from the angle <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sa</span></span>, to the
angle <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>ea</span></span>. The angles are given in 360
degree to maxint ratio form. Uses the current color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure farc([var f: text;]
x1, y1, x2, y2, sa, ea: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw a filled arc. Draws a filled
arc on an ellipse, whose bounding box is <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span> to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>. The arc starts on the ellipse
from the angle <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sa</span></span>, to the
angle <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>ea</span></span>. The angles are given in 360
degree to maxint ratio form. Uses the current color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure fchord([var f:
text;] x1, y1, x2, y2, sa, ea: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw a filled cord. Draws a
filled cord on an ellipse, whose bounding box is <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span> to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>. The cord starts on the
ellipse from the angle <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sa</span></span>, to the angle
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>ea</span></span>. The angles are given in 360
degree to maxint ratio form. Uses the current color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure ftriangle([var f:
text;] x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw a filled triangle. Draws a
filled triangle given the three points <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>, and <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x3</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y3</span></span>.
Uses the current color and mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure cursorg([var f:
text;] x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Position the cursor graphically.
Moves the cursor to the pixel position <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span>
and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function baseline[(var f:
text)]: integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find baseline of current font.
Finds the baseline, or line on which all characters of the current font sit
upon, in terms of offset from the character bounding box origin in y.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure setpixel([var f:
text;] x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set single pixel. Sets a single
pixel at the point <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span>,<span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>, using the current color and
mode.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure fover[(var f: text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set overwrite mode foreground.
Sets all new drawing to overwrite old colors on the foreground.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure bovver[(var f:
text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set overwrite mode background.
Sets all new drawing to overwrite old colors on the background.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure finvis[(var f:
text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set invisible mode foreground. Sets
all new drawing to discard colors on the foreground.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure binvis[(var f:
text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set invisible mode background.
Sets all new drawing to discard colors on the background.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure fxor[(var f: text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set xor mode foreground. Sets all
new drawing to xor old colors with new colors on the foreground.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure bxor[(var f: text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set xor mode background. Sets all
new drawing to xor old colors with new colors on the background.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function chrsizx[(var f:
text)]: integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find character size in x. Returns
the x size, in pixels, of the characters in the current font. If the font is
proportional, its x size will vary per character. The size will then be the
space character, which is guaranteed to be the widest character in the font.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function chrsizy[(var f:
text)]: integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find character size in y. Returns
the y size, in pixels, of the characters in the current font.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function fonts[(var f: text)]:
integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find number of fonts. Returns the
number of fonts installed on the system.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure font([var f: text;]
fc: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Select logical font. Selects a
font by logical number <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>fc</span></span>, where <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>fc</span></span> is 1..fonts.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure fontnam([var f:
text;] fc: integer; var fns: string);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find name of logical font.
Returns the name of the logical font <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>fc</span></span>
in the string <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>fns</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure fontsiz([var f:
text;] s: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set font size. Sets the height of
the current font, in pixels.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure chrspcy([var f:
text;] s: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set character y spacing. Sets the
line to line spacing to s, in pixels.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure chrspcx([var f:
text;] s: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set character x spacing. Sets the
character to character spacing <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>s</span></span>,
in pixels.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function dpmx[(var f: text)]:
integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find dots per meter x. Finds the
dots per meter in x of the current display device.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function dpmy[(var f: text)]:
integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find dots per meter y. Finds the
dots per meter in y of the current display device.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function strsiz([var f: text;]
s: string): integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find pixel size of string. Finds
the total x size of the string <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>s</span></span>,
in pixels. Accounts for all sizes and spacing.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function chrpos([var f: text;]
s: string; p: integer): integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find pixel offset of character.
Finds the pixel offset from the start of a string <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>s</span></span>
in terms of x pixels, to the character by the index <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>p</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure writejust([var f:
text;] s: string; n: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Write string justified. Writes
the given string s into the number of x pixels n. If the space is more than is
required, the extra space will be distributed between the characters. If the
space given is insufficient for the characters to be drawn, it will be drawn in
the minimum amount of space for the entire string.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function justpos([var f:
text;] s: string; p, n: integer): integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find justified pixel off set of
character. Finds the pixel offset from the start of a string <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>s</span></span>, in terms of x pixels, to the
character by the index <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>p</span></span>, for a string
justified to fit into <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>n</span></span> pixels. The
rules of justification are the same as for writejust.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure condensed([var f:
text;] e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set condensed mode. Sets the
current font to occupy a shorter baseline than normal. Note that this effect
may not be implemented.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure extended([var f:
text;] e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set extended mode. Sets the
current font to occupy a longer baseline than normal. Note that this effect may
not be implemented.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure xlight([var f:
text;] e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set extra light mode. Sets the
current font to extra light printing. Note that this effect may not be
implemented.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure light([var f: text;]
e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set light mode. Sets the current
font to light printing. Note that this effect may not be implemented.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure xbold([var f: text;]
e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set extra bold mode. Sets the
current font to extra bold printing. Note that this effect may not be
implemented.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure hollow([var f:
text;] e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set hollow mode. Sets the current
font to hollow, or sunken look, printing. Note that this effect may not be
implemented.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure raised([var f:
text;] e: boolean);.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set raised mode. Sets the current
font to raised, or relief look, printing. Note that this effect may not be
implemented.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure settabg([var f:
text;] t: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set graphical tab. Sets a tab to
the pixel <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>t</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure restabg([var f:
text;] t: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Reset graphical tab. The tab at
pixel <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>t</span></span> is removed. If no tab is set at
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>t</span></span>, no error will result.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure fcolorg([var f:
text;] r, g, b: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set foreground color graphical.
The foreground color is set to the red <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>r</span></span>,
green <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>g</span></span> and blue <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>b</span></span> color. The colors are in
0..maxint ratios, with 0 = black, and maxint = saturated. The nearest color to
the one given is found and set active as the foreground color. The exact color
that results will depend on the total number of colors implemented in the
system, and could well be black and white in a system so equipped.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure bcolorg([var f:
text;] r, g, b: integer);.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Set background color graphical.
The background color is set to the red <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>r</span></span>,
green <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>g</span></span> and blue <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>b</span></span> color. The colors are in
0..maxint ratios, with 0 = black, and maxint = saturated. The nearest color to
the one given is found and set active as the foreground color. The exact color
that results will depend on the total number of colors implemented in the
system, and could well be black and white in a system so equipped.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure loadpict([var f:
text;] p: integer; fn: string);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Load picture. Loads the picture
from the filename fn to logical picture number <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>p</span></span>,
which is 1..n. The file must be in a format that the system understands, and is
converted to an in memory format that is optimal for the system, such as a direct
match for the graphics device in use.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function pictsizx([var f:
text;] p: integer): integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find picture size x. Returns the
size, in pixels of x, of the logical picture <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>p</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>function pictsizy([var f:
text;] p: integer): integer;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Find picture size y. Returns the
size, in pixels of y, of the logical picture <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>p</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure picture([var f:
text;] p: integer; x1, y1, x2, y2: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Draw picture. The logical picture
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>p</span></span> is drawn into the bounding box
formed by <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x1</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y1</span></span>
to <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x2</span></span>,<span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y2</span></span>.
The picture is stretched or compressed as required to do this. The method used
to stretch or compress may depend on how much computing power is available on
the system. There is no attention paid to aspect ratio.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure delpict([var f:
text;] p: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt'>Remove logical picture from use.
The logical picture <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>p</span></span> is removed
from the picture queue, and will no longer take up memory space.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure scrollg([var f:
text;] x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:21.6pt'>Scroll in arbitrary directions.
The screen is scrolled according to the differences in <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span> and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>,
which are in pixels. Uncovered areas on the screen appear in the current
background color.</p>

<span style='font-size:11.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Times New Roman","serif";
mso-fareast-font-family:Calibri;mso-fareast-theme-font:minor-latin;mso-bidi-theme-font:
minor-bidi;mso-ansi-language:EN-US;mso-fareast-language:EN-US;mso-bidi-language:
AR-SA'><br clear=all style='mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:
always'>
</span>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;line-height:115%'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading7><a name="_Toc251442736"><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:
none;text-underline:none'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>G<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Annex
G: Windows Management Library</a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span> is
completely upward compatible with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span>
and <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span>. Given a single fixed
screen, <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span> sudivides the screen into
vitual windows, which can be set to any size or position. A <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span> compatible program sees
its window as a &quot;virtual screen&quot;, and does not know that some or all
of the contents of that screen may be hidden. A <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
aware program can participate in the benifits of a windowed environment.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442737"></a><a name="_Toc221641180"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442737'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.1<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Screen Appearance</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The idea of windows management is to take a program that
thinks it is talking to an ordinary terminal, and allow the presentation of
multiple such windows within a single screen.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>By default, <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
emulates a terminal interface for each window that is identical to the behavior
of a full screen window from the programs' point of view. A standard size
screen is implemented for the program of 80 characters by 25 lines (even if the
real display has a different size). <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
accepts program writes, and places that information in a buffer that looks like
the ideal screen. Then, the contents of that buffer are placed on the screen in
various arrangements to complete the desktop for the user.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442738"></a><a name="_Toc221641181"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442738'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.2<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Window Modes</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A window can be any actual size on the display. A window can
also be off the display entirely, so that it accepts changes to its buffer, but
does not display those changes. In addition, a window can be active, inactive,
or overlapped, or even completely covered by other windows on the display.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442739"></a><a name="_Toc221641182"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442739'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.3<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Buffered Mode</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A window comes up by default in the &quot;buffered&quot;
mode. In buffered mode, the program has a &quot;virtual display surface&quot;
that it writes to. <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
maintains this surface in memory, and maintains the actual onscreen window as a
scrolled window on that. The program is unconcerned with what part of its
screen is being displayed, or even if it is displayed at all. The user operates
the window using the frame controls.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Buffered mode is designed to allow the program to be
unconcerned with the management of the display. However, the program can set
the size of the virtual display using <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sizbuf[g]</span></span>.
When a buffer is resized, its contents is cleared to the current background
color.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The buffer is a display surface that the buffer handler
keeps for the program. The buffered mode allows a program to &quot;think&quot;
it has a window of size N, but the appearance of the window on the actual
display is a window on that virtual display that is maintained by the buffer
handler.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Because from the programs point of view, the window size
does not change, and is always the complete window, the resize events are
performed transparently to the program. Similarly, the minimize and maximize
events are handled for the program (see <!--[if supportFields]><span
style='mso-element:field-begin'></span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>REF _Ref221850706 \r \h <span
style='mso-element:field-separator'></span><![endif]-->G.4<!--[if gte mso 9]><xml>
 <w:data>08D0C9EA79F9BACE118C8200AA004BA90B02000000080000000E0000005F005200650066003200320031003800350030003700300036000000</w:data>
</xml><![endif]--><!--[if supportFields]><span style='mso-element:field-end'></span><![endif]-->).
Discovery is when a window or part of a window is uncovered on the display.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When in buffered mode, the window manager is may use scroll
bars to display within a screen view that is smaller than the buffer.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Although buffered mode automatically handles window status
events (covered in <!--[if supportFields]><span style='mso-element:field-begin'></span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>REF _Ref221850919 \r \h <span
style='mso-element:field-separator'></span><![endif]-->G.4<!--[if gte mso 9]><xml>
 <w:data>08D0C9EA79F9BACE118C8200AA004BA90B02000000080000000E0000005F005200650066003200320031003800350030003900310039000000</w:data>
</xml><![endif]--><!--[if supportFields]><span style='mso-element:field-end'></span><![endif]-->),
these events are still sent through. The program normally ignores them.
However, on a very fast system, this allows a program to keep buffered mode
active, but still respond to window management requests such as resize. The
meaning of these events is given next.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442740"></a><a name="_Ref221850919"></a><a
name="_Ref221850706"></a><a name="_Toc221641183"><span style='mso-bookmark:
_Ref221850706'><span style='mso-bookmark:_Ref221850919'><span style='mso-bookmark:
_Toc251442740'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;
mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.4<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Unbuffered Mode</span></span></span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The program can leave buffered mode by executing <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>buffer</span></span>. Unbuffered mode has no
display buffer, and no default actions for events. Instead, the onscreen
appearance of the window is set dynamically by the program.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When unbuffered mode is entered, the buffer for the window
is discarded, and it becomes entirely up to the program to manage its own
window by watching events. The size of the window no longer refects the buffer,
but instead is the size of the onscreen window. In contrast to buffered mode,
this can change many times as the window is resized under user control.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Buffered mode can be reentered at any time. The contents of
the buffer are cleared to spaces.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When running unbuffered, it is assumed that the program will
manage the update of the on-screen display surface itself. When running
unbuffered, the program handles events that are normally handled automatically.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredraw</span></span> event
contains the limits of an update rectangle that shows what part of the client
area needs to be redrawn on the screen. The program can ignore the update
rectangle, and simply redraw the entire screen, or it may only redraw the
figures that overlap the update rectangle. The latter is usually more time
efficient. <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredraw</span></span> gives its update
rectangle in character coordinates. There is also a pixel version of that, <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredrawg</span></span>, which uses pixel
coordinates. On a graphical system, both messages will be sent, and they
duplicate each other. The character coordinate <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredraw</span></span>
will contain a rectangle of characters that at least covers the pixels in a
graphical system. Since <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredraw</span></span> and <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredrawg</span></span> duplicate each other,
only one of them should be obeyed, and the other ignored.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etresize</span></span> event
indicates that the onscreen window has changed its size. Its purpose is to let
the program update any calculations based on the size of the window. This event
can be ignored, used to update stored <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>maxx[g]</span></span>
and <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>maxy[g]</span></span> values, or it could even
cause the entire arrangement of the screen to be reformed.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etresize</span></span> is not
normally used to redraw areas of the screen. If a resize event causes new
screen area to be uncovered, then one or more <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredraw</span></span>
events will also be sent. There is no way to determine exactly which redraw
operations correspond to the resize event, so some redundancy is possible with
applications that repack their window's contents on a resize.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etmin</span></span> event
indicates that the window has been minimized. When a window is minimized, it
will not be displayed, and won't receive <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredraw</span></span>
events. On the desktop, the window is represented by a small icon. An
alternative form of minimization is for the window to receive an <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etmin</span></span> message, followed by a <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etresize</span></span> message, and continued <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etredraw</span></span> messages. This a hint
for the window to display vital information in a much smaller format that fits
into the minimized icon.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etmax</span></span> event
indicates that the window has been maximized, or covers the entire desktop.
Typically, this requires no action on the part of the program.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etnorm</span></span> event
indicates a normal window mode has been resumed from <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etmin</span></span>
or <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etmax</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442741"><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:
none;text-underline:none'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.5<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Defacto
transparency</a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Using unbuffered mode, it is possible to achieve window
transparency on most systems. Transparency means that some parts of the window
will show through to the display surface underneath the window. This can be
used to implement advanced effects like non-rectangular windows, and it is the
basis for widgets (which appear in <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>widlib</span></span>).
For defacto transparency to work, the drawing order of windows must be tightly
controlled.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442742"></a><a name="_Toc221641184"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442742'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.6<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Delayed Window Display</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a window is created, it is not displayed until an
actual write operation is made to it. This allows the window to be changed in
configuration by the program before it is displayed, and prevents the window
being rapidly changed on screen as that happens. For example, the program might
want to take the window out of buffered mode, change its size, remove the
frame, or add menus.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442743"></a><a name="_Toc221641185"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442743'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.7<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Window Frames</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Under most window systems, each window has a &quot;window
frame&quot;, which has various window management functions. These include:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l20 level1 lfo18'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='font-family:Symbol;mso-fareast-font-family:Symbol;mso-bidi-font-family:
Symbol'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>·<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Move bars to resize and move the window.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l20 level1 lfo18'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='font-family:Symbol;mso-fareast-font-family:Symbol;mso-bidi-font-family:
Symbol'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>·<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>A title bar that indicates what program is
running in the window.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l20 level1 lfo18'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='font-family:Symbol;mso-fareast-font-family:Symbol;mso-bidi-font-family:
Symbol'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>·<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>A &quot;minimize&quot; button.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l20 level1 lfo18'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='font-family:Symbol;mso-fareast-font-family:Symbol;mso-bidi-font-family:
Symbol'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>·<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>A &quot;maximize&quot; button.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpLast style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l20 level1 lfo18'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='font-family:Symbol;mso-fareast-font-family:Symbol;mso-bidi-font-family:
Symbol'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>·<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>A &quot;menu&quot; bar.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a window is in buffered mode, none of the frame
features are under the control of the program, but instead are managed by the
buffering software.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The appearance, or even the existence, of any of these items
can be customized by the program. The frame can be enabled or disabled by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>frame</span></span>. The title can be set by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>title</span></span>. The title, minimize and
maximize buttons are considered part of the &quot;system bar&quot;, which can
be enabled or disabled by <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sysbar</span></span>. The
resize bars are enabled or disabled by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sizable</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The frame control functions <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>frame</span></span>,
<span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>title</span></span>, <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sysbar</span></span>
and <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sizable</span></span> are optional within an
implementation. The system may not allow an application to, for example,
disable its sizing bars, or it may not have a set of system controls on the
window. The program must be ready for these commands not to have an effect. For
example, after the sizeable function is set false, it should still be ready to
receive a new size. If, for example, the size of the graphic to be presented is
a fixed size, it would be filled out or clipped to fit the resulting window.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442744"></a><a name="_Toc221641186"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442744'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.8<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Multiple Windows</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>If the output file appears in the program header, then that
is output as the main window by default. A window can also be explicitly opened
by <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>openwin</span></span>. When a new window is
opened, it is placed on the desktop with the other windows. The text files <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>inw</span></span> and <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>outw</span></span> specify the input and output
files attached to the window. The input file receives all user input and events
from the window, and all of the write and other drawing operations are sent to
the output file. The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>id</span></span> is a number,
from 1 to N, that specifies the logical window. The logical window number 1 is
reserved for the standard input and output pair, even if it is not specifically
opened, since it can be opened by the program at any time.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The output side of a window must allways be unique, but the
input side can be shared. Multiple windows can be attached to a single input
file, and that input file will receive all of the input and events from all
attached windows. The logical window number is returned with each event, so
that the source of the input or event can be determined.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To completely decouple separate windows, another thread is
used to run the separate window. This can be done, for example, to create a
widget library that does not depend on the event handler of the calling module.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Windows can be closed using the standard Pascaline <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>close</span></span> call. Only the output side
of a window is used to close that window. The input side is automatically
closed when there is no longer a window that references it.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442745"></a><a name="_Toc221641187"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442745'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.9<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Parent/Child Windows</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Windows systems are tree structured, and each window is a
child of a parent window, with the exception of a &quot;root&quot; or master
window, which is usually the window that contains the entire desktop. So far,
we have been creating windows that live side by side on the desktop, and have
the desktop as their parents. However, it is possible to nest windows within
each other.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A window is opened as a child by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>openwin</span></span>,
which is the same <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>openwin</span></span> call
with a parent specified. The parent file <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>par</span></span>
is the text file that is attached to the output window of the parent. To be a
child a a window means to move as one within it. It maintains its position
within the parent, even as the parent itself moves. It allways is in front of
the Z ordering for the parent, but behind any other siblings of the parent. It is
minimized, maximized and closed with the parent.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>All windows have their position given in relative terms to
the parent's client area origin. Any position operation is also relative to the
parent.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The common use of the parent is to create
&quot;sibling&quot; windows, or windows equivalent to the default program
window in status, that are positioned independently within the parent. A
program starts as the child of an external parent window. This could be an
actual program, such as a program acting as a manager, or it could be the
desktop root. There is no difference between these for the program.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442746"></a><a name="_Toc221641188"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442746'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.10<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Moving and Sizing Windows</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Moving a window is done with the <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>setpos[g]</span></span>
procedure. The position is given in parent coordinates. When a window is on the
desktop, it is necessary to find the size of the desktop, which is found with <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scnsiz[g]</span></span>. When the desktop size
is returned for character sizes, this is calculated using the current character
sizes for the window. This is for comparitive purposes only. The desktop may
use a completely different set of characters and sizes.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Sizing a window is done with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>setsiz</span></span>
procedure. This sets the size of the entire window, and so does not indicate
the resulting size of its client area. To find out what window size is needed
for a given client area, before actually sizing the window, the function <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>winclient[g]</span></span>. It returns the
window size needed to contain that client.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The mode set declaration is:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>{ windows mode sets }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>winmod = (wmframe,<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>{ frame on/off }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span>wmsize,<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>{ size bars on/off }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span>wmsysbar); { system bar on/off }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>winmodset = set of winmod;</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To find the current size of a window, in parent terms, the
procedure <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>getsiz[g]</span></span> is used. This procedure
is useful when you need to find the size of a window on the desktop.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442747"></a><a name="_Toc221641189"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442747'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.11<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Z Ordering</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The Z ordering, or back to front ordering of windows, is
determined by default to be newest created windows to the front, oldest to the
back. This order can be modified by the users when they select windows towards
the back to come to the front. The program can also reorder windows at will by
sending them to the front or back. Note that the Z ordering is always relative
within a parent.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To send a window to the front, the call <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>front</span></span> is used. To send a window
to the back, the call <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>back</span></span> is used.
To achieve a specific order within a set of windows, they should be send to the
front in the opposite order from which they are to appear, ie, the backmost
first, and the frontmost last. Alternately, the windows can be sent to the back
in the order they appear.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>There are other reasons besides reordering windows that
these functions might be usefull. When an error in encountered, it is common to
send a window containing the error message to the front of the parent Z order,
and to send the entire window to the front. Placing an active display or a
background pattern in a frameless maximized window can create wallpaper.
Placing the same window in the back can be a screen saver.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442748"></a><a name="_Toc221641190"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442748'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.12<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Class Window Handling</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span> handles
windows as a set of two files, the input and output.However, these don't need
to allways be a set. It is possible to reuse an already open input file as the
input side of any new window. This is possible because the user id supplied
when the window is open is passed with all messages to the event procedure. </p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Allowing a single program thread to handle multiple open
windows allows the creation of multiple window programs without the need to
create a multitask program. For each message, the id is examined, and the
action performed on the appropriate output file.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Because the output file is unique, it is allways the handle
used to refer to the window in management calls.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442749"></a><a name="_Toc221641191"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442749'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.13<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Parallel Windows</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Parallel tasking is a natural match to a multiwindowing
system. With a task created to operate each window, the program is simplified,
and user response is generally greater.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To increase the responsiveness of user interfaces, the
recommended ideal program design for windowing programs is to create two tasks
for each window, the &quot;foreground&quot; and the &quot;background&quot;
tasks. The foreground task performs the event loop and the redraw, resize, move
and other user interface tasks. The background task would handle computation
tasks and other tasks related to performing actions or changing the drawing
surface. The foreground task determines if an action that requires the
background task is required, then sends a command to the background task via a
monitor queue.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The reason for this construction is that the things the user
sees, such as keeping the client area redrawn, or obeying a move, resize,
minimize or similar command allways have a task waiting to perform them. This
means that elementary user desktop management appears responsive to the user,
while data manipulation and comutation tasks simply delay client area updates.
Users will be much more willing to tolerate client area slowdowns than having a
window apparently lock stubbornly in position.. Ideally, the client area should
also have an indication that computation is taking place. The most modern
method for this is a progress indicator that gives estimates as to when a task
will complete, if the task will take longer than about 1 second.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A parallel forground task is automatically provided for a
buffered mode window. It needs to be created in the case of an unbuffered
window.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>If the work performed in a client area is trival, only a
foreground task need be provided. But be aware that it is very easy to slip
into a mode where essential updates are held off. Calling a file procedure,
waiting on a timer, or other simple task compromises the response time for
window management. Better to leave the window in buffered mode, or structure
with foreground/background from program creation than to have to add it later.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442750"></a><a name="_Toc221641192"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442750'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.14<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Menus</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The menu is a series of buttons labeled with text for user
action. The buttons can either have a direct effect on the program, or they can
activate a series of submenus in a tree structure.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The exact location of a menu is system dependent. It may or
may not affect the client area.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A menu is described to <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
by constructing a data structure:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>menuptr = ^menurec;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>menurec = <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>record</b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>             </span>next:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>menuptr; { next menu item in list }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>             </span>branch:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>menuptr; { menu branch }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>             </span>onoff:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>boolean; { on/off highlight }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>             </span>oneof:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>boolean; { &quot;one of&quot; highlight }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>             </span>bar:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span>boolean; { place bar under }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>             </span>id:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>integer; { id of menu item }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>             </span>face:<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>pstring<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'> 
</span>{ text to place in button }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span><b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>;</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Menu buttons are highlighted when pointed to. Additionally,
a button can have &quot;on/off&quot; highlighting. In this mode, a state is
kept for the button that is flipped on or off as the button is pressed. The
highlighting for on and off states is different. The data structure that
defines a menu is not used or updated after it is used to create a menu.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>onoff</span></span> field
true selects on/off highlighting. After the menu is activated, the state of the
button can be changed with <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>menusel</span></span>. The
button state is not automatically changed by a user menu select. The program
must specifically change the state of the button.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Another highlight mode is &quot;one of&quot; or
&quot;checklist&quot; highlighting. In this case, a group of related buttons
are joined by setting the <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>oneof</span></span> flag on
each item in the list but the last. The highlighting for a button that is
selected is different from one that is not, and only one item will be active in
the list.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Like on/off buttons, user selection does not automatically
change the state of the buttons in the list. It must be specifically changed by
a <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>menusel</span></span> call. </p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Typically, niether on/off nor one/of switching is available
for top level (horizontal) menus, and the program should not count on them.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Menu items can be grouped in a vertical list by setting the <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>bar</span></span> field active. This causes a
horizonal bar to be drawn under the menu item. Vertical lists are described
next in &quot;menu sublisting&quot;.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A button can be enabled or disabled. A disabled button is
highlighted specially, such as greyed out. It indicates a button that is
entirely inactive, usually because that function is not available. This is done
to allow the same menu to be used regardless of the availability of the
functions on the menu. Also, some functions come and go. For example, a
&quot;save&quot; button (for &quot;save file&quot;) might only be active if the
file has changed, and needs to be saved.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The enable status can be changed after the menu is activated
by <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>menusel</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Each button in a menu can have a numeric <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>id</span></span>. This is used by several functions
to change the state of buttons. It is an error to have two buttons with the
same id.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>face</span></span> text
string indicates what text will appear on the face of the button. The system
will automatically size the buttons to fit the largest text of a button, so
care should be taken not to have a single button's text so long as to cause a
lot of white space in the menu.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442751"></a><a name="_Toc221641193"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442751'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.15<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Setting Menu Active</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A menu is constructed by the program as a list using the
menu data structure, then set active by calling <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>menu</span></span>,
which can also turn the menu back off.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a menu data structure is activated, all of the fields
are copied and placed into an internal form. After the activation, the menu
data has no function, and changing its fields will have no effect. The menu
data structure can be recycled immediately after the <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>menu</span></span>
call.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442752"></a><a name="_Toc221641194"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442752'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.16<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Setting Menu States</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>For an on/off button, the procedure <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>menuena</span></span> is used to enable or
disable the button after it has been placed in a menu. For oneof highlighting,
the procedure <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>menusel</span></span> is used. This removes any
other select active, and either sets the given button active, or no button
active.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442753"></a><a name="_Toc221641195"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442753'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.17<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Standard Menus</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Besides presenting a menu in the method standard for the
implementation, it is also likely that there exists a standard arrangement of
commonly used buttons in the menu. manlib defines a series of such standard
buttons.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>{ standardized menu entries }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smnew<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>       </span>= 1; { new file }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smopen<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>= 2; { open file }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smclose<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span>= 3; { close file }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smsave<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>= 4; { save file }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smsaveas<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>= 5; { save file as name }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:
FR'>smpageset<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>= 6; { page setup }<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='mso-ansi-language:
FR'>smprint<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span>= 7; { print }<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=ES-CO
style='mso-ansi-language:ES-CO'>smexit<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     
</span>= 8; { exit program }<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=ES-CO
style='mso-ansi-language:ES-CO'>smundo<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     
</span>= 9; { undo edit }<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smcut<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>       </span>= 10; { cut selection }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smpaste<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span>= 11; { paste selection }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smdelete<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>= 12; { delete selection }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smfind<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>= 13; { find text }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smfindnext<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>= 14; { find next }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smreplace<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>= 15; { replace text }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smgoto<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>= 16; { goto line }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smselectall = 17; { select all
text }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smnewwindow = 18; { new window }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smtilehoriz = 19; { tile child
windows horizontally }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smtilevert<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>= 20; { tile child windows vertically }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smcascade<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>= 21; { cascade windows }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smcloseall<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>= 21; { close all windows }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smhelptopic = 22; { help topics }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smabout<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span>= 23; { about this program }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>smmax<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>       </span>= 23; { maximum defined standard menu entries
}</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>{ standard menu selector }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>stdmenusel = <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>set</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>of</b> smnew..smmax;</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Standard menu button definitions should be used whenever
possible. To create a menu using standard menu buttons, the procedure <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>stdmenu</span></span> is used. The arrangement
of the menu is choosen to match the implementation, and the non-standard
buttons provided are integrated into the menu in the normal place for the
implementation. When standard buttons are used, the button ids are set to the
values shown above, so these values should not be used by the program.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Note that <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>stdmenu</span></span> simply
constructs a menu, it does not set it active.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442754"></a><a name="_Toc221641196"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442754'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.18<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Menu Sublisting</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When there is not enough room to represent the entire menu
on a window, or anytime the size of a menu must be compressed, the tree
structure of menus can be used with &quot;pulldown&quot; menus. A pulldown menu
is a vertical menu list that appears under the selected button. This is done by
placing a submenu in the <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>branch</span></span> pointer
of the menu structure. The branch defines the pulldown menu items. The branch menu
item is specially indicated to show that it is a branch, and not an immediate
button, usually with an arrow pointing towards where the branch pulldown will
appear. Any number of levels of pulldown menus can be created. The levels under
the topmost branch are generally placed to the right of the branch button.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>If a branch pulldown cannot be placed where it should, due
to the proximity of the button to the edge of the display, it instead goes back
in the other direction, up or down, or both, until space is found for it. If
the entire pulldown cannot be displayed, it is truncated. This would only
happen if the pulldown was to large for the display.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a branch button is pressed, no event is generated for
it. The button is strictly used to activate the pulldown.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442755"></a><a name="_Toc221641197"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442755'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.19<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Advanced Windowing</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The features of a window besides the client area are
designed to be the minimum features implemented across platforms. To implement
more advanced windows appearances with custom menus, controls and status lines,
the standard method is to turn off frames and menus for child windows, and use
them as building blocks for more advanced client areas with multiple subwindows
and features.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>It is recommended that at least one menu appear for a
program, even if the functions duplicate some of the advanced controls you
provide. The reason for this is that the menu has different presentation methods
in different systems, so providing the standard &quot;top&quot; menu will help
your programs' portability.</p>

