style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>etredraw:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'> 
</span>(rsx, rsy, rex, rey:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>integer); {
redraw screen }<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>etmenus:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>(menuid:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>              </span>integer); {
menu item<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>                                                 
</span><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>selected }<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>etup, etdown, etleft, etright, etleftw,
etrightw, ethome,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span></span><span lang=FR style='font-size:
10.0pt;mso-ansi-language:FR'>ethomes, ethomel, etend, etends, etendl, etscrl,
etscrr,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='font-size:
10.0pt;mso-ansi-language:FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>etscru, etscrd, etpagd, etpagu, ettab,
etenter, etinsert,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='font-size:
10.0pt;mso-ansi-language:FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>etinsertl, etinsertt, etdel, etdell, etdelcf,
etdelcb, etcopy,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='font-size:
10.0pt;mso-ansi-language:FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>etcopyl, etcan, etstop, etcont, etprint,
etprintb, etprints,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='font-size:
10.0pt;mso-ansi-language:FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>     </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>etmenu, etterm, etframe, etresize, etmin,
etmax,<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span lang=FR style='font-size:
10.0pt;mso-ansi-language:FR'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span></span><span
style='font-size:10.0pt'>etnorm: (); { normal events }<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>{ <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>end</b>
}<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='font-size:10.0pt'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=Code0><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style='font-size:
10.0pt'>end</span></b><span style='font-size:10.0pt'>;<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.21<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>WIndow Objects</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>All of the procedures, functions and other declarations in <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span> are also available in a
graphical terminal object. of the form:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>class window;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>var input, output: text; { input
and output files for this screen }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>    </span>er: evtrec;<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span>{ event record }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>{ procedures and functions from
manlib }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The complete catalog of procedures and functions from gralib
are available as methods in the graphicalterminal class. There are minor
differences, which are detailed with the procedure and function descriptions in
<!--[if supportFields]><span style='mso-element:field-begin'></span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>REF _Ref251353925 \r \h <span
style='mso-element:field-separator'></span><![endif]-->E.16<!--[if gte mso 9]><xml>
 <w:data>08D0C9EA79F9BACE118C8200AA004BA90B02000000080000000E0000005F005200650066003200350031003300350033003900320035000000</w:data>
</xml><![endif]--><!--[if supportFields]><span style='mso-element:field-end'></span><![endif]-->.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>graphicalterminal</span></span>
object is instantiated, it’s input and output files are automatically linked to
the standard <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>input</span></span> and <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>output</span></span> files of the program.
Thus, where specifying the input and output files in <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span> procedures and functions
is optional, in methods of the <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span>
object these files <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style='mso-bidi-font-style:
normal'>must not</i></b> be specified.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The input and output files used by the <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span> object are made available
as class members input and output. This allows them to be used in non-method
procedures (such as read and write).</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span> object
also contains an event record, so it is not necessary to specify an external
event record in the <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>event</span></span>
procedure.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span> object
can be created as follows:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>program p;<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>joins manlib;<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'>var</b> ti: instance of gralib.graphicalterminal;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>begin</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>ti.home; { send cursor to home position }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>writeln(ti.output, ‘hello, terminal world’);</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>repeat { event loop }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>ti.event; { get next event }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>      </span>{ process events }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  
</span>until ti.event = etterm { loop until program cancelled }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>end.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Where <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>terminal</span></span> is the
terminal object. Terminal objects can be instantiated statically or
dynamically.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>Terminal</span></span>
objects contain their own state for the following:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>1.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Location of cursor.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>2.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Current attributes and colors.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>3.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Current font and size.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>4.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Buffer working/display status.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>5.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Tab stops.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>6.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Timer numbers.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>7.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Cached pictures.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>8.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Buffer/non-buffer mode.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>9.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Window attributes, size and location in parent.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>10.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Z-order.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpLast style='margin-left:54.0pt;mso-add-space:auto;
text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l15 level1 lfo25'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>11.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Menus.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Window objects are inherently separate drawing surfaces
(unlike <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>terminal</span></span> and <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>GraphicalTerminal</span></span> objects). The <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>OpenWin</span></span> procedure must be invoked
to activate a window object. Unlike the <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
module procedure, <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>OpenWin</span></span> does
not specify an output file, this is inherent to the object. The <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>input</span></span> file is optional, and is
used to bond multiple windows to a single input handler.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When multiple terminal objects exist, and all have the
cursor visibility on, the cursor will remain at the last place it was
specifically directed to. This can mean that the cursor will rapidly appear to
swap back and forth between the active drawing terminal objects. This can be
improved by only selecting one of the terminal objects as having a visible
cursor. Typically, a the cursor should be placed to attract user attention to
where text is being entered, or if none is being entered, it should be turned
off.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Note that <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>winlib</span></span> also
exposes a terminal object that is identical to the <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span>
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>terminal</span></span> object and the <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span> <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>GraphicalTerminal</span></span> object. It
features the same calls as <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span>, but
does not allow access to the advanced features in <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442757"></a><a name="_Toc221641199"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442757'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>G.22<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Procedure and Functions in manlib</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>For all of the following calls, If the screen file <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span> is not present, the default is
the standard output file. If the procedure or function is a method, neither the
input nor output screen file should be specified, since it is inherent in the
object.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>OpenWin</span></span>
procedure is an exception, and both the module procedure and the <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span> method have special
formats.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure openwin(infile,
outfile [, parent]: text; id: integer); { module procedure }</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure openwin(infile
[,parent]: text; id: integer); { window object method }</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Opens a new window. The input file <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>infile</span></span> will get messages
pertaining to the window, and the output file <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>outfile</span></span>
will be used to draw to it. The input file may already be open elsewhere, in
which case it will get all messages for all open windows opened with it. The
window will be placed at 1,1 within the parent and held out of display. The
output file is always used as the window handle, since it unique to the window.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>The window identifier <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>id</span></span>
is a number from 1 to n that is returned in messages concerning the window.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Only the output side of a window pair is used in
procedures or functions that operate on the window once opened with openwin.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>A window is closed with the standard Pascaline close
procedure, used on the output file for the window. The input side of the window
is automatically closed when there are no longer any windows that reference it.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'>If
the optional parent window is specified, the new window is created as a child
of the given parent. This means that it will always be displayed within the
parent, and be clipped to it.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'>When
used as a method in the <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>window</span></span> object,
the output file is not specified.</p>

<p class=Code0 style='mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;page-break-after:
avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure buffer([var f:
text;] b: boolean);</p>

<p class=Code0 style='mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;page-break-after:
avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Engages or removes window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>
from buffered mode, according to the boolean <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>b</span></span>.
In buffered mode, all of the drawing for a window is performed on a memory
buffer, then copied to the screen. The screen view of the buffer can be all or
part of the buffer, and multiple buffers can be managed.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure sizbuf[g]([var f:
text;] x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:
normal'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Sets the size of the buffer used to draw into. <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span> and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>
indicate the width and height, respectively, of the buffer surface in window <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>. It is an error if buffering is
not enabled. Sizbuf sets sets the buffer size in characters. Sizbufg sets the
buffer size in pixels.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.0cm;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure title([var f: text;]
view s: string): integer;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;page-break-after:
avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Sets the title of the window <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span> to the string <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>s</span></span>. If the title is too long for
the current window size, an implementation defined method will be used to make
it fit, for example, it is clipped.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure frame([var f: text;]
e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Enables or disables the appearance of the frame in
window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>, according to boolean <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>e</span></span>, <span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>which includes the minimize, maximize, title,
size, move and close controls. If the frame is removed, the user will be unable
to operate the frame controls.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>It is possible that the implementation may not allow
removal of the frame. In this case, all of the frame controls will still be
active, and the program must accept these.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure sysbar([var f:
text;] e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Enables or disables the system control bar for a window
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>. If <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>e</span></span>
is true, the system bar is enabled, otherwise disabled. The system bar normally
includes the title, minimum, maximum, and other control buttons. If the frame
is not enabled, then the system bar will not appear regardless of the sysbar
status, but it will be recorded.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>It is possible that the implementation may not allow
removal of the system bar. In this case, all of the system controls will still
be active, and the program must accept these.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure sizable([var f:
text;] e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Enables or disables the sizing bars for a window <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>. If <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>e</span></span>
is true, the sizing bar is enabled, otherwise disabled. If the frame is not
enabled, then the size bars will not appear regardless of the sizeable status,
but it will be recorded. If the size bars are removed, the user will be unable
to resize the window.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>It is possible that the implementation may not allow
removal of the sizing bars. In this case, all of the size controls will still
be active, and the program must accept these.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure setpos[g]([var f:
text;] x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Sets the position, within the parent, of a child window
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>, using position <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span> and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>.
If the window is on the desktop, then the window position is relative to the
desktop. The <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>setpos</span></span> procedure sets the
position in terms of characters, and the <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>setposg</span></span>
procedure set the position in terms of pixels.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>The position is set in terms of the parent's
coordinates. The mode of the parent's coordinates may not be known. For
example, the desktop may not even have a character mode, so the idea of a
character size may be arbitrary. What matters in this case is the relative
position and size in the desktop as determined by scnsiz.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure scnsiz[g]([var f:
text;] var x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Finds the size of the user screen or desktop for window
<span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span> to the size <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span> and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>.
<span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scnsiz</span></span> returns the size in
character terms, and <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scnsizg</span></span> returns
the size in pixel terms. If the desktop does not have a character mode, an
arbitrary scale is created. What is important is the relative location within
the desktop.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure winclient[g]([var
f: text;] cx, cy: integer; var wx, wy: integer; ms: modset);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Determines the window size needed for a given client
size within window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>. Given a
desired client size of <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>cx</span></span> and <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>cy</span></span>, in width and height, the
necessary window size to achieve that will be returned in <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>wx</span></span> and <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>xy</span></span>.
<span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>winclient</span></span> determines these
measurements in character demensions, and <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>winclientg</span></span>
determines them in pixel terms.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>The set of modes <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>ms</span></span>
is used to determine the needed window size.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>If the parent of the window has no character mode, then
one is created that will be acceptable to <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>setpos</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure getsiz[g](var f:
text; var x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Finds the size of a window in parent coordinate terms
for window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>, to size <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span> and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>.
<span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>getsiz</span></span> returns the character
size, and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>getsizg</span></span> returns the pixel size.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>If the parent has no character mode, then one is
created that is compatible with other <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
functions and procedures.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure setsiz[g]([var f:
text;] var x, y: integer);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Sets the size of window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>
in parent coordinate terms, to size <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>x</span></span>
and <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>y</span></span>. <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>setsiz</span></span>
sets the character size, and <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>setsizg</span></span> sets
the pixel size.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>If the parent has no character mode, then one is
created that is compatible with other manlib functions and procedures.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure back[(var f: text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Sends the window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>
to the back of the parent Z order.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure front[(var f: text)];</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Sends the window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>
to the front of the parent Z order.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure menu([var f: text;]
m: menptr);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;page-break-after:avoid'>Sets up
the menu bar for window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span> from the
given list, which contains a menu bar definition structure. </p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>If the menu pointer is nil, then the menu is removed.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>The menu data structure is copied during the call, so
the menu structure can be reused or freed.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure stdmenu(sms:
stdmenusel; var sm: menuptr; pm: menuptr);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together'>Contructs
a standard menu and returns that in <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sm</span></span>.
<span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sms</span></span> contains the set of desired
standard buttons. <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>pm</span></span> contains a
menu containing non-standard buttons to be added to the menu. The menu is
constructed using the desired standard buttons, and the non-standard buttons
placed into the menu at a standard location.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure menuena([var f:
text;] id: integer; e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Enables or disables a on/off menu button for window <span
class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>. <span class=ReferenceChar0><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>id</span></span>
refers to a button <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>id</span></span> that was
specified in the menu data structure. If <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>e</span></span>
is true, the button is enabled, otherwise disabled. The highlighting of the
button will change to match.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'>procedure menusel([var f:
text;] id: integer; e: boolean);</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:36.0pt;mso-pagination:widow-orphan lines-together;
page-break-after:avoid'>Selects a button from a oneof list to be active in
window <span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>f</span></span>. <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>id</span></span>
refers to a button id that was specified in the menu data structure. If <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>e</span></span> is true, the button is
selected, otherwise deselected. All other buttons in the oneof list are
deactivated. The highlighting of the buttons will change to match.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;line-height:115%'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

</div>

<span style='font-size:11.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Times New Roman","serif";
mso-fareast-font-family:Calibri;mso-fareast-theme-font:minor-latin;mso-bidi-theme-font:
minor-bidi;mso-ansi-language:EN-US;mso-fareast-language:EN-US;mso-bidi-language:
AR-SA'><br clear=all style='page-break-before:right;mso-break-type:section-break'>
</span>

<div class=Section11>

<p class=MsoHeading7><a name="_Toc251442758"><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:
none;text-underline:none'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>H<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Annex
H: Widget Library</a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span> and <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span> define terminal and
graphical operations on a fixed screen. <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
defines its division into &quot;virtual windows&quot;. The last part of the
puzzle are the elements placed within those windows to allow user control.
These include buttons, sliders, scroll bars, checkboxes, and similar user
interface elements.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>These are sometimes referred to as controls or widgets.
Dialogs are predefined windows with widgets in them. What these user elements
have in common is they all use <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
elements to define the window they appear in, and <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span>
or <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span> routines to draw their
appearance. </p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>wiglib</span></span> can be
performed entirely in terms of <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>manlib</span></span>
with <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>gralib</span></span> or <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>trmlib</span></span> calls. However, it is
still dependent on a particular operating system because of its appearance. <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>wiglib</span></span> is your key to attaining
the &quot;look and feel&quot; of a particular operating system. But it is still
possible to write your own widgets.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442759"></a><a name="_Toc221641219"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442759'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.1<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Tiles, Layers and Looks</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Each widget is implemented in its own window. A layout of
widgets occurs when several widgets are tiled side by side, or place on top of
each other (layered). For example, a window surface with text and scrollbars to
control the positioning of that text is done by constructing a series of
windows.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The first window is the window that holds the presented
text. The second and third are the windows that hold the horizontal and
vertical scrollbars.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Layering is done by defacto transparency. A series of
widgets are placed one atop the other. For example, a text window can be laid
ontop of a background window.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The key to interface design is to think of a window as
simply a building block for construction of the user interface.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Widgets are fundamental to the &quot;look&quot; of an
interface. Because <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>wiglib</span></span> uses the
native widgets on the operating system it serves, you will pick up quite a bit
of that look from just the use of the widgets.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Certainly its true that there is more to an application than
just the look of the widgets. There are layout conventions, actions, and other
intangables. The rule that applies to Pascaline portability is:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l8 level1 lfo19'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='font-family:Symbol;mso-fareast-font-family:Symbol;mso-bidi-font-family:
Symbol'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>·<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Pascaline should be able to target a high
percentage of simple applications just by use of its normal <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>wiglib</span></span> components.</p>

<p class=MsoListParagraphCxSpLast style='margin-bottom:10.0pt;mso-add-space:
auto;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l8 level1 lfo19'><![if !supportLists]><span
style='font-family:Symbol;mso-fareast-font-family:Symbol;mso-bidi-font-family:
Symbol'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>·<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Pascaline should be able to finish a high
percentage of the work to create complex applications.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Think of interface design as performed by a collection of
wooden blocks. Certainly any shape of building design can be performed, but the
result won't be exactly the same as the equivalent building. What makes a
building look &quot;custom&quot; are the final touches of plaster and paint
that give it a finished look.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The typical cycle in designing an Pascaline application is
to design an inital version that uses just wiglib components, then finish the
design with special layouts, actions and colors for a particular operating
system that give it the &quot;look and feel&quot; of a native application.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442760"></a><a name="_Toc221641220"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442760'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.2<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Background colors and placement</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Widgets are placed by specifiying their &quot;bounding
box&quot; or rectangle. This is the rectangle that contains the wiget. A widget
can occupy all of the specified bounding box, or just a part of it. Typically,
the operating system will try its best to format the widget to fit within the
space provided.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The color scheme for widgets can vary. However, widgets are
designed to be placed against a background color that is system dependent. This
is available as a new system defined color, backcolor. It can be selected by
the standard color selection routines.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>type</b> color = (black,
white, red, green, blue, cyan,</p>

<p class=Code0><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>              </span>yellow,
magenta, backcolor);</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>fcolor(backcolor);</p>

<p class=Code0>bcolor(backcolor);</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>There are many ways to group widgets so that they blend into
your background. The surface they appear on can be specifically colored using
the background color, or you can create a child window with that color. Also,
there is a background widget that can color the background for widgets
automatically.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442761"></a><a name="_Toc221641221"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442761'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.3<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Sizes</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Using widgets can go a long way towards getting the look and
feel of a system in an independent way. The other important factor for
achieving system independence is to control sizing of widgets. For each widget,
with the exception of dialogs, there is a size routine. The size routine takes
the particular features of that widget, such as face text or borders, and gives
the best size for the widget, given the desired client area, contents, etc..</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size information must be considered against the
particular widget to be created. Sizing is key to establishing the layout of
widgets in a window, and key to producing a truly portable application.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442762"></a><a name="_Toc221641222"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442762'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.4<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Logical Widget Identifiers</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A widget is created with a logical number, from 1 to n,
where n is a positive integer. You specify the number you wish to create the
widget under. The id of a widget cannot be the same as any other active widget,
but widget logical identifiers can be reused by killing the widget first.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442763"></a><a name="_Toc221641223"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442763'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.5<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Killing, Selecting, Enabling and Getting Text to
and from Widgets</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A widget is killed by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>killwidget</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Some widgets can be selected, which changes their appearance
to th select state. A widget is selected by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>selectwidget</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A selected widget can be a checkbox that is checked, a radio
button that is pushed, or similar effect. The processing of selects, and
keeping track of the state of widgets is up to you. However, this is a very
flexible system. You can implement widgets that flip their state when clicked
on, a series of widgets that are mutually exclusive, and many other
combinations.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Similar to selection, widgets can be enabled or disabled by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>enablewidget.</span></span> Widgets are
disabled by default. When a widget is disabled, it has the disabled appearance,
such as greyed out. The widget will not give click events when it is disabled.
Disabling a widget is typically used when it is not needed or not available in
the current context. For example, a &quot;next&quot; button could be disabled
when there is no next item to process.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Some widgets have text that can be set, read, or in some
cases, both. The text in a widget is set by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>putwidgettext</span></span>.
The text in a widget can be read by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>getwidgettext</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Setting and getting the widget text is used with widgets
that allow the user to modify the text, such as an edit box.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442764"><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:
none;text-underline:none'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.6<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Resizing
and repositioning a widget</a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To prevent the need to remove and replace widgets each time
a window is resized, <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>sizwidget[g]</span></span>.
To reposition the widget in the parent window, <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>poswidget[g]</span></span>
exists.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442765"></a><a name="_Toc221641224"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442765'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.7<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Types of widgets</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Widgets come in three different types, controls, components
and dialogs. Controls are widgets that the user can manipulate, and these
widgets issue events to the program that owns them.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Components are display widgets whose only job is to form
part of a display to the user. A group box, and a background are examples of
components. Some components have active displays, such as the progress bar.
However, components never issue events.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Dialogs are fully autonomous windows that exist apart from
the applications windows. They can be very complex inside, having a whole
system of layout, widgets and other features. They resemble entirely separate
programs.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Dialogs take a series of parameters when they are called,
and deliver those same parameters back to the caller, with any modifications
the user performs on the data.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442766"><![if !supportLists]><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:
none;text-underline:none'><span style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.8<span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><![endif]>Z
ordering</a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>widlib</span></span> uses
“implicit Z ordering” for layered widgets. In order for widgets to properly
layer, the drawing Z order must be controlled. For example, a background must
be drawn first, followed by whatever is on top of it, or the drawing of the
background will wipe out what is on the surface of the background.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>To enable this, widlib makes sure that widgets which are
designed to be layered appear first in the drawing order.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442767"></a><a name="_Toc221641225"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442767'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.9<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Controls</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A button can be created with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>button[g]</span></span>.
The button is drawn in the specified rectangle with a label text string, and <span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>logical widget number <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>id</span></span>. The text string will be a
single line of text with no control characters, and will be presented on the
face of the button. The font style and size will be the same as other buttons
in the operating systems user interface.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a button is pressed, it will typically change its
appearance to indicate that. The button will send an event <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etbutton</span></span>. This event carries the
id of the button that was asserted. Similarly, when a button is released, it
changes appearance back from the pressed state.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Buttons can be any size, any height and width. The button
face text does not get larger with the button, but remains centered. However,
if the button too small, some of the face text will be clipped off. The size of
the button can be determined before creating it by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>buttonsiz[g]
</span></span>and height, respectively, of the button.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Buttons cannot be selected, but they can be disabled. The
text in a button can be neither read nor written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A checkbox is created with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>checkbox[g]</span></span>.
when hit, give a single event that indicates activation, <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etchkbox</span></span>. This event contains the
id of the widget.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Checkbox sizing is found with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>checkboxsiz[g]</span></span>.
Checkboxes are sized to minimum, but since they have no edges (like a button),
there is typically no need to add space to them.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Checkboxes can be selected (checked). They can be enabled or
disabled, and default to enabled. They cannot have their face text changed or
read.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Radio buttons work identically to checkboxes, but have a
different appearance. A radio button is created by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>radiobutton[g].</span></span>
Radio buttons, when hit, give a single event that indicates activation, <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etradbut</span></span>. This event contains the
id of the widget.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Radio button sizing is found with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>radiobuttonsizsiz[g]</span></span>,
Radio buttons are sized to minimum, but since they have no edges (like a
button), there is typically no need to add space to them.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Checkboxes can be selected (checked). They can be enabled or
disabled, and default to enabled. They cannot have their face text changed or
read.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Scrollbars are placed vertically by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scrollvert[g]</span></span>. Scrollbars are
placed horizontally by <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scrollhoriz[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Scroll bars can be placed using any demensions, but the
width of a vertical scroll bar, and the height of a horizontal scroll bar
usually has a standard size. This can be determined by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scrollvertsiz[g].<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A user movement of a scrollbar is given by the event <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etsclpos</span></span>. This event does not
move the scrollbar slider. This must be done by the program via <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scrollpos</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When a user positions the scroll bar directly, it will
follow the users mouse movements. However, it will return to the original
position unless the <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etsclpos</span></span> event
is responded to and a <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scrollpos</span></span> call
is made.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Besides position events, scrollbars issue two types of
button pushes, referred to as line and page button events. The line buttons are
the arrow buttons at either side of the scroll bar. The page buttons are the
space between the scrollbar slider and the line arrows. A press anywhere in
this area generates a page button event.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The page button area may not appear at all if the slider is
fully to one side of the scrollbar.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The page and line terminology for these buttons occurs
because their most common use is to scroll documents. In this case, the line
buttons would be used to move the document one line up or down. In the case of
horizontal scrollbars, this would be one character left or right (despite the
term &quot;line&quot; in the buttons name). The page refers to one screenful of
text, in any direction. If the page up buton is hit, for example, the document
would move one screenful up.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>It's up to the program to implement the actions for line
up/down and page up/down. In fact, these events can be used for any purpose you
want in your programs.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Besides the position of the slider, its size can also be
controlled by <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scrollsiz</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The standard use of the scrollbar size is to indicate what
proportion of the document or display is contained within the onscreen display,
vs. the total size of the document. For example, if the document has 50% of its
total displayed, then the size of the scrollbar is set to %50, which is done
by:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>scrollsiz(f, n, maxint div 2);</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Scroll bars cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or have
face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A number can be selected in an edit box by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>numselbox[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The first number that appears in the number select box is by
default the lower bound.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Number select boxes are an easy way to enter numbers from
the user, and automatically restrict the input to numbers only. The numbers are
entered in decimal, and cannot be negative. The edit box allows the number to
be directly edited. Also, there are usually up and down buttons that allow the
user to count the number up or down by one.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a number select box is found by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>numselboxsiz[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Number select boxes cannot be selected, enabled or disabled,
or have face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A general string can be editted with <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>editbox[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>And empty edit box is placed, and the user has the ability
to edit text into the box, with cursor movements, character delete, etc.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>An edit box can be presented blank, or default text can be
placed into the edit box. If the user presses enter to the box, it sents a <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etedtbox</span></span> event. However, the
program can use any method to signal done, such as a button next to the edit
control. The resulting text can then be retrived from the edit box.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of an edit box is found by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>editboxsiz[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Edit boxes cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or have
face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A list box is a series of items that can be selected. It is
placed with <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>listbox[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The string list definition appears as:</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>{ string set for list box }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>strptr = ^strrec;</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'>strrec = record</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>next:
strptr; { next entry in list }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span><span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'> </span>str:<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'> 
</span>pstring { string }</p>

<p class=Code0 style='page-break-after:avoid'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>end;</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The string pointer is a list of strings, each string of
which describes an entry in the list box.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>When the user selects an item from the list box, the <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etlstbox</span></span> event is returned. This
event gives the id of the widget, and the number of the select, from the top.
The first item in the list will be 1, the second 2, etc.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a list box is found by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>listboxsiz[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>List boxes cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or have
face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The same multiple string selection can be done in a
different way by <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>dropbox[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A drop box only shows the whole list if the user selects it,
otherwise only the currently selected entry is shown. The full list &quot;drops
down&quot; from the selection box when the user selects it. A drop box takes
less space than a list box when it is not selected. A drop box selection is
signaled by the etdrpbox event, which gives the widget id, and the number of
the selection, from 1 to n.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a drop box is found by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>dropboxsiz[g]</span></span>. Because drop boxes
have two appearances, one when open, and one when closed, both sizes are
returned. The closed appearance gives the basic size of the widget, but the open
size makes is possible to determine if the list will go beyond the edge of the
window when open.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Drop boxes cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or have
face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Very similar to a drop box, a drop/edit box allows selection
from a list, but also allows the current selection string to be editted.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A drop/edit box is placed with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>dropeditbox[g]</span></span>.
When a selection is made from the drop/edit box, the <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etdrebox</span></span>
event is sent, which includes the widget identifier. The selection data itself
is a string, and must be retrived with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>getwidgettext</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span
style='font-family:"Arial","sans-serif";mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a drop/edit box is found by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>dropeditboxsiz[g]</span></span>. Because
drop/edit boxes have two appearances, one when open, and one when closed, both
sizes are returned. The closed appearance gives the basic size of the widget,
but the open size makes is possible to determine if the list will go beyond the
edge of the window when open.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Drop/edit boxes cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or
have face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Sliders are linear controls that can be placed either
horizontally or vertically.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A vertical slider is placed with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>slidevert[g].</span></span>
A horizontal slider can be placed by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>slidehoriz[g].</span></span><b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style='font-family:"Arial","sans-serif";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'><o:p></o:p></span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Sliders indicate changes in their position with the event <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>etsldpos</span></span>. This gives the widget
id, and a <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>maxint</span></span> ratioed position of the
slider, from 0 to <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>maxint</span></span>. 0 is
the top or leftmost position of the slider, and <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>maxint</span></span>
is the bottom or rightmost position of the slider.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a slider can be determined by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>scrollvertsiz[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Sliders cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or have
face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Tab bars allow the user to select from a series of labeled
tabs, usually to specify locations in<span style='mso-spacerun:yes'> 
</span>document. A tabbar is a group box with tabs on one, two, three or four
edges. The tabs can be placed on the top, bottom, left or right side of an
included client area.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A tabbar is placed by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>tabbar[g]</span></span>.
</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Tabbar selections are indicated by the event <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>ettabbar</span></span>, which gives the widget
id, the side which generated the event and the tab number selection, from 1 to
n, counting from the first string entry in the list.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The orientation of a tabbar is given by:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>{ orientation for tab bars }</p>

<p class=Code0>type tabori = (totop, toright, tobottom, toleft); </p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a tabbar is found by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>tabbarsiz[g]</span></span>.
A tabbar acts like a group box, and has a client area to place child windows or
widgets. The required client size can be specified, and the sizing call returns
the offset required to find the client location within the tabbar.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>If the tabbar must fit into a fixed window size, the size of
the resulting client for a tabbar can be found with <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>tabbarclient[g</span></span>].</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Tab bars cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or have
face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442768"></a><a name="_Toc221641226"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442768'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.10<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Components</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A background box is placed by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>background[g]</span></span>.
A background box is designed to serve as the background to a series of
controls, and it has the standard color for such backgrounds.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Group boxes have no sizing, because there are no borders or
other content. They are just a colored rectangle. Tab bars cannot be selected,
enabled or disabled, or have face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A group box is similar to a background box, but it has a
label for the &quot;group&quot; of controls contained within it. It is placed
by <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>group[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a group box found by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>groupsiz[g]</span></span>.
A group box has a client area to place child windows or widgets. The required
client size can be specified, and the sizing call returns the offset required
to find the client location within the group box.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Group boxes cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or have
face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A progress bar is used to indicate the progress of a job
completion, like installing software, saving a file, etc.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>It is placed by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>progbar[g]</span></span>.
The initial progress indication is zero when placed. The size of the progress
bar is set by <span class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>progbarpos</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The size of a progress bars can be determined by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>progbarsiz[g]</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Progress bars cannot be selected, enabled or disabled, or
have face text read or written.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoHeading8><a name="_Toc251442769"></a><a name="_Toc221641227"><span
style='mso-bookmark:_Toc251442769'><![if !supportLists]><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:Arial;text-decoration:none;text-underline:none'><span
style='mso-list:Ignore'>H.11<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span></span><![endif]>Dialogs</span></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A dialog is a completely separate window which is
preformatted with widgets. Dialogs introduce complex queries into a program,
using the look of the native operating system,.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>Dialogs display a property known as modality. Since the
dialog is a separate window, it can be independent of the other windows created
by the calling task, or the dialog can be forced to appear at the top of the
applications stacking order.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span class=ReferenceChar0><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:
"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>widlib</span></span> uses two
modes of modality. If the task that created the dialog also created other
windows, the dialog is forced to the front of the other windows, and selection
of the other task windows is disabled. This indicates to the user that only the
dialog is currently being managed by the task.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>If windows are created by different threads, then the dialog
will not be modal vs. the other thread’s windows. This reflects the fact that
the windows outside the dialog can run while the dialog does.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>An alert dialog is used to send errors or other important
messages to the user. It has a window title, a message that constitutes the
alert, and typically has an &quot;ok&quot; or &quot;close&quot; button for the
user to indicate they have seen it.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>An alert is created by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>alert.</span></span>
The alert call will not return until the user has clicked the ok button for the
alert.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The query dialogs allow the user to select important
information such as a file, a search string, or color or a font. They use a
model called &quot;flow through&quot;. The query may select several types of
information, and will accept a default setting, allow the user to select a new
setting, and return that. The flow through model means that several parameters
are set up before the call, may or may not be modified by the query, then are
returned to the caller. The program stops until the dialog is completed by the
user.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>All of the parameters of a dialog may or may not be
implemented in the actual system dialog. The flowthrough system allows for
differences in systems. Since the variables are preinitalized with the
defaults, if the dialog does not implement a particular parameter, the value
will be left unchanged.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A color can be choosen by <span class=referenceChar><span
style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>querycolor.
</span></span>The default color is set before the call, and the possibly
changed color is returned by the call.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A file to open name is selected by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>queryopen</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The default filename is passed in as a dynamic string, and a
different string is returned as the result of the dialog. The string returned
is different than the input string, and the program is responsible for
disposing of both strings. If the dialog is canceled instead of completed by
the user, the string returned is zero length. This means to not proceed with
the open operation.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A file to save name is selected by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>querysave</span></span>. </p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The default filename is passed in as a dynamic string, and a
different string is returned as the result of the dialog. The string returned
is different than the input string, and the program is responsible for
disposing of both strings. If the dialog is canceled instead of completed by
the user, the string returned is zero length. This means to not proceed with
the save operation.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A string to search for is selected by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>queryfind</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The option flags are given by a set of flags:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>{ settable items in find query }</p>

<p class=Code0>qfnopt = (qfncase, { Case sensitive }</p>

<p class=Code0><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span>qfnup},<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>  </span>{ Search up/Search down }</p>

<p class=Code0><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span>qfbre);</p>

<p class=Code0>qfnopts = <b style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>set</b> <b
style='mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>of</b> qfnopt;</p>

<p class=Code0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The default search string is passed in as a dynamic string.
The string returned is different than the input string, and the program is
responsible for disposing of both strings. If the dialog is canceled instead of
completed by the user, the string returned is zero length. This means to not
proceed with the search operation.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>A string to search for and replace is selected by <span
class=referenceChar><span style='mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";
mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi'>queryfindrep</span></span>.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal>The option flags are given by a set of flags:</p>

<p class=MsoNormal><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=Code0>{ settable items in replace query }</p>

<p class=Code0>qfropt = (qfrcase, { case sensitive }</p>

<p class=Code0><span style='mso-spacerun:yes'>          </span>qfrup,<span
style='mso-spacerun:yes'>   </span>{ search up/search down }</p>

